Time complexity: O(m × n) 
Auxiliary space complexity: O(m × n) 


class Solution {
    private void dfs(int sr,int sc, int color, int[][] image, int orgColor){

        int m=image.length;
        int n=image[0].length;

        //Boundary and color check
        if(sr<0 || sc<0 || sr>=m || sc>=n || image[sr][sc]!=orgColor)
            return;
        
        //Recolor 
        image[sr][sc]=color;


        dfs(sr-1,sc,color,image,orgColor);
        dfs(sr+1,sc,color,image,orgColor);
        dfs(sr,sc-1,color,image,orgColor);
        dfs(sr,sc+1,color,image,orgColor);
    }
    public int[][] floodFill(int[][] image, int sr, int sc, int color) {

        int orgColor=image[sr][sc];

        if(orgColor==color)
            return image;

        dfs(sr,sc,color,image,orgColor);
        return image;
    }
}



Algorithm steps
1. Store the starting pixel's original color in originalColor.
2. If originalColor == color, return the image immediately because no changes are required.
3. Start DFS from (sr, sc).
4. Check whether the current coordinates are outside the image or the current pixel's color differs from originalColor. If either condition holds, return.
5. Change the current pixel's color to the new color.
6. Recursively explore all four directions: up, down, left, and right.
7. Continue until all connected pixels with the original color have been recolored.
8. Return the modified image.
